   "band": "Band 3",
   "posted": "2026-10-29",
   "expires": "2026-11-08",
   "skills": [],
   "description": "<p> <strong>NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde (NHSGGC) </strong>is one of the largest healthcare systems in the United Kingdom, employing approximately 40,000 staff across a wide range of clinical and non-clinical professions and roles. We deliver acute hospital, primary, community and mental health services to a population of over 1.15 million people, and to a wider population of 2.2 million when regional and national services are included. </p><p><b> The role: </b></p><p> As a Health Care Support Worker within the Burns and Plastic Surgery service, you will work as part of a multidisciplinary team providing high-quality patient care in support of registered nursing staff. The role involves delivering direct patient care, supporting clinical procedures, and contributing to a safe, compassionate, and efficient care environment. You will regularly work independently while having access to guidance and support from qualified nursing staff. </p><p><br></p><p> NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde (NHSGGC) may be able to provide visa sponsorship for certain roles, subject to the post meeting the relevant eligibility criteria. Sponsorship is not guaranteed and will be determined on a case-by-case basis following interview and during the pre-employment verification process. Applicants must state their current right to work in their application. </p><p> Please see below for more details: </p><p> Skilled Worker visa and Health and Care Worker visa </p><p> <b> Duration, Location, and Working Pattern: </b> </p><p> Permanent Role </p><p> Glasgow Royal Infirmary </p><p> 36 Hours Per Week (12 hour rotational inclusive of weekends, nights and public holidays. Core shifts if service requires) </p><p> <b> Key responsibilities: </b> </p><p> As part of the multidisciplinary team, you will provide practical and clinical support within the Burns and Plastic Surgery Unit, ensuring patients receive high-quality, person-centred care. You will assist patients with activities of daily living, undertake technical and clinical procedures appropriate to the role, monitor and report changes in patient condition, maintain accurate patient records, and support the effective delivery of nursing care. The role also involves maintaining a safe environment for patients, visitors and staff, contributing to the management of resources and equipment, supporting wound care and nurse-led clinics, and working collaboratively with colleagues, patients and their families to achieve the best possible outcomes. </p><p><i>Shortened here. The full description is on the original advert.</i></p>",
   "inUkOnly": false,
