   "expires": "2026-11-08",
   "skills": [],
   "description": "<p>Elmwood Ward is a specialist dementia ward at Parklands Hospital. We are seeking a compassionate Carer's Peer Support Worker with lived experience of supporting a relative or friend experiencing mental distress.</p><p>Using your lived experience, you will provide hope, understanding & practical support to families & carers of patients receiving inpatient mental health services. You will build trusting relationships, offer one-to-one support, facilitate carer groups & help carers access information, services & community resources.</p><p>Working closely with carers, patients & the multidisciplinary team, you will develop Carer's Communication Plans, promote communication & ensure carers feel heard, valued & involved in care wherever possible.</p><p>You will advocate for carers, promote the Triangle of Care, strengthen links with local organisations & help improve the support available to carers. You will also contribute to service improvement & evaluation.</p><p>We are looking for someone with excellent communication skills, empathy & a passion for supporting others through shared experience. You will be able to build rapport, maintain professional boundaries, work collaboratively & empower carers to recognise their strengths, build resilience & access support.</p><p>This is an exciting opportunity to make a difference to carers & families while helping shape & improve services on Elmwood Ward. If this sounds like you, we would love to hear from you.</p></p> <h4>Main duties of the job</h4> <p><p>The role of Carer's Peer Support Worker has been developed specifically for people who have lived experience of caring for a friend or family member experiencing mental distress. Through sharing wisdom from their own experiences, Carer's Peer Support Workers will inspire hope and belief that recovery is possible in others.</p><p>The Carer's Peer Support Worker works collaboratively and aims to improve the wellbeing of families and carers of people who use Acute Inpatient Mental Health Services. The post holder will support carers directly, provide support to carers' groups, develop and maintain relationships with service provider teams, and contribute to service development.</p><p>The post holder will work in partnership with families and carers, multidisciplinary teams, and statutory, voluntary and community-sector organisations to support recovery, communication and access to appropriate support.</p></p><p><i>Shortened here. The full description is on the original advert.</i></p>",
