   "expires": "2026-11-01",
   "skills": [],
   "description": "<p> <strong>NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde (NHSGGC) </strong>is one of the largest healthcare systems in the United Kingdom, employing approximately 40,000 staff across a wide range of clinical and non-clinical professions and roles. We deliver acute hospital, primary, community and mental health services to a population of over 1.15 million people, and to a wider population of 2.2 million when regional and national services are included. </p><p> <b> The role: </b> </p><p> An exciting opportunity has arisen for motivated and compassionate Healthcare Support Workers to join the Renal Outpatient Service at the Queen Elizabeth University Hospital (QEUH). The successful candidates will work as part of a multidisciplinary team, supporting registered nursing staff in delivering safe, effective, and person-centred care to patients with kidney disease. The Renal Outpatient Department provides specialist care across the patient pathway, including pre-dialysis care, dialysis preparation and commencement, kidney transplant assessment, and post-transplant follow-up care. </p><p><br></p><p> This role does not meet the eligibility criteria for a Skilled Worker visa or a Heath and Care Worker visa. NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde (NHSGGC) is unable to provide candidates or employees with a Certificate of Sponsorship (COS). </p><p> <b> Duration, Location, and Working Pattern: </b> </p><p> 2 x Permanent Roles </p><p> Queen Elizabeth University Hospital </p><p> 2 x 18 Hours Per Week (Worked over 3 Days - Monday - Friday) </p><p> <b> Key responsibilities: </b> </p><p> The post holder will provide both clinical and administrative support within the Renal Outpatient and Peritoneal Dialysis Services. Duties will include undertaking venepuncture, cannulation, ECG recording, and assisting with the preparation and set-up of peritoneal dialysis equipment. The role involves maintaining accurate digital patient records and supporting the delivery of high-quality patient care. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to infection prevention and control standards and contribute to maintaining a safe clinical environment. The role is physically demanding and includes moving and handling patients with varying levels of dependency, handling ward equipment, spending the majority of the shift walking or standing, and working in an environment where there may be exposure to body fluids and therapeutic products. </p><p><i>Shortened here. The full description is on the original advert.</i></p>",
