   "description": "<p><strong>Join Our Team as a Children's Residential Support Worker</strong></p><p>As a Residential Support Worker, you will play a key role in providing consistent, high-quality care and support to children and young people living within the home. You will help create a safe, nurturing environment, support young people in their daily lives, and contribute to the achievement of their individual care plans and goals.</p><p><strong>Key Responsibilities of a Children's Residential Support Worker</strong></p><ul><li>Build positive relationships with children and young people.</li><li>Provide day-to-day care, guidance and emotional support.</li><li>Promote independence, resilience and positive outcomes.</li><li>Support education, hobbies, appointments and community activities.</li><li>Safeguard children and report concerns appropriately.</li><li>Complete records and reports accurately.</li><li>Work closely with colleagues, families and external professionals.</li><li>Be a positive role model and help create a safe, nurturing home environment.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Shift Patterns</strong></p><p>The role operates on a rota basis and includes long-day shifts with sleep-ins to provide continuity of care for the children and young people we support.</p><p>Our shifts run from <strong>10:00am to 10:00pm</strong>, followed by a sleep-in from <strong>10:00pm to 7:00am</strong>, then <strong>7:00am to 10:30am</strong> the following morning. Handover takes place between <strong>10:00am and 10:30am</strong>.</p><p>Each shift includes <strong>15.5 working hours plus a sleep-in</strong>.</p><ul><li>A full-time contract is <strong>37.5 hours per week</strong>, which works out at approximately <strong>2.5 shifts per week</strong>, averaged across our 3-week rolling rota.</li></ul><p>Flexibility to work evenings, weekends and bank holidays is essential.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Qualifications and training</strong></p><p>The postholder is required to hold, or to achieve within two years of starting in the role, the Level 3 Diploma for Residential Childcare (England) or an accepted equivalent.</p><p>This requirement arises directly from the Children's Homes (England) Regulations 2015 and is a condition of working in a registered children's home rather than an optional development opportunity.</p><p><i>Shortened here. The full description is on the original advert.</i></p>",
